Q: How can I cut a string after X characters? A: <p>Modern style variation of <a href="/a/18146354/#51307037">Steve Brush's answer</a>, i.e. '…' only if
string was really clipped, <strong>plus</strong> avoid hassles if accidentally a non-string is passed:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const clip_value = ( value, max_length ) =&gt; {
  if( String( value ).length &lt;= max_length ) return String( value ) ;
  return String( value ).slice( 0, max_length ) + '\u2026' ;
}
</code></pre>

Q: Custom resizable HashMap problem in JavaScript, A: <p>Since <code>buckets</code> is reassigned at the end of the <code>resize</code> method, the <code>hash</code> method is working with the old number of buckets, which causes key-value pairs to be inserted into the new buckets at the wrong indexes. This can be fixed by reassigning <code>buckets</code> before inserting the previous key-value pairs.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>resize() {
    const oldBuckets = this.buckets;
    this.buckets = Array.from({length: oldBuckets.length * 2}, () =&gt; []);
    for (const bucket of oldBuckets)
        for (const [key, value] of bucket)
            this.buckets[this.hash(key)].push([key, value]);
}
</code></pre>
